"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["45510"],{48196(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-847","slug":"prime-rate","term":"Prime Rate","also_known_as":[],"difficulty":"Intermediate","definition":"The prime rate is the interest rate that commercial banks in the United States and some other countries charge their most creditworthy customers, used as a benchmark for many other loans.","explanation":["In the United States, the prime rate moves closely with the Federal Reserve\'s policy rate. Most major US banks set their prime rate at about 3 percentage points above the upper limit of the federal funds target range, and change it whenever the Federal Reserve changes rates.","Many variable-rate loans are priced as the prime rate plus a margin. Examples include credit cards, home equity lines of credit, some business loans and some student loans. For example, if the prime rate is 7.5%, a credit card might charge prime plus 14%, or 21.5%.","Because of this link, when the Federal Reserve raises or lowers interest rates, the cost of many consumer and business loans in the United States changes almost immediately.","Canada also uses a prime rate, set by its major banks and linked to the Bank of Canada\'s policy rate. Many other countries do not use a prime rate in the same way. In the United Kingdom, for example, many variable-rate loans and mortgages are linked to the Bank of England\'s Bank Rate or to a lender\'s own standard variable rate."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"primary-market","term":"Primary Market"},"next":{"slug":"principal","term":"Principal"}}')}}]);